{
  "evidence": "TEXT_G: Secure a full national debate and a fair referendum on Britain’s relationship with Europe; ask voters to choose between a federal Europe and an association of sovereign nations; hold the referendum before or with the next General Election; criticize main parties for offering only a single-currency referendum and insist on addressing sovereignty. TEXT_F: NHS guarantee with real‑terms increases and more staff; national Respite Care Programme and social services reforms; regenerate the worst estates and protect the environment; universal nursery vouchers for all four‑year‑olds and education standards/interventions; more police, tougher sentencing, and faster justice; defend the UK with strong Armed Forces and NATO; keep taxes low, welfare into work, resist EU social/employment powers; partnership of sovereign nation states in Europe and a referendum on single currency.",
  "reasoning": "Given the electorate’s high weight on Care and Equality, TEXT_F aligns better by offering extensive care‑oriented commitments (NHS, carers, estate regeneration) and some equal‑treatment measures (universal nursery vouchers), while TEXT_G focuses narrowly on sovereignty and process. Both address Authority/Loyalty, but TEXT_F balances these with Care and proportionality without emphasizing Sanctity, matching the profile’s priorities more closely.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_F"
}
